% ============================================================================
% quadrilateral-secondary-property — for the quadrilaterals where the SAME
% source states a SECOND property in the same defining sentence, that
% second property (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `quadrilateral-types.adj`
% (`quadrilateral_property(shape, property)`, ONE defining property per
% quadrilateral: square, rectangle, rhombus, parallelogram, trapezoid). That
% table's `property` column has room for only the PRIMARY defining property;
% the SAME already-quoted MathWorld sentences also state a SECOND, distinct
% property for two of the five quadrilaterals that never made it into a row:
%
%   rectangle → opposite_sides_equal_length
%     "A closed planar quadrilateral with opposite sides of equal lengths a
%      and b, and with four right angles."
%   parallelogram → opposite_angles_equal
%     "A parallelogram is a quadrilateral with opposite sides parallel (and
%      therefore opposite angles equal)."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? quadrilateral_secondary_property(rectangle, $Property)   % opposite_sides_equal_length
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `quadrilateral-types.adj`'s own `quadrilateral_property`): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `quadrilateral_secondary_property(shape,
% property)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query — the engine returns the secondary property WITH its
% source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on square,
% rhombus, and trapezoid, whose own already-cited spans in
% `quadrilateral-types.adj` state only the single primary property, with no
% second property to decode here.
%
% Only TWO of `quadrilateral-types.adj`'s five quadrilaterals have a
% genuinely second, distinct property in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table
% is deliberately narrow rather than inventing a property the source does
% not state. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a property and recall
% which quadrilateral it describes:
%
%     ? quadrilateral_secondary_property($Shape, opposite_angles_equal)   % parallelogram
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME rectangle span already
% quoted inside `quadrilateral-types.adj`'s own provenance block, from the
% SAME MathWorld "Rectangle" page that table already cites — no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a part of each
% already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope
% (per `quadrilateral-types.adj`'s own convention), so `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the rectangle span; the parallelogram row's own span is
% listed above for independent auditability. `trust authoritative` — the
% same tier `quadrilateral-types.adj` already carries (Wolfram MathWorld, a
% primary mathematics reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table quadrilateral_secondary_property {
    columns shape, property

    row (rectangle, opposite_sides_equal_length)
    row (parallelogram, opposite_angles_equal)

    source "A closed planar quadrilateral with opposite sides of equal lengths a and b, and with four right angles."
    locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Rectangle.html"
    trust authoritative
}
